@style(IDocDemo)
@title(Code 3: code from files, includes and snippets)

Code can live in its own files beside the document, in this document's sidecar folder.

@section(@@path for code)

`@@path(code: ...)` lists the folders searched for code files, in order; a file in none of them is an error that names every place looked.

@path(code: 03-files-and-includes.assets, 03-files-and-includes.assets/sv)

@section(A whole file)

`@@code(file=...)` shows a file as a listing; add `run` to run it.

@code(file=stats.py, run)
@end

@section(@@codex: a file, its language from the suffix)

@codex is the older name for a runnable file. `src=` (also `file=`, `path=`, `source=`) names it, `lang=`/`language=` overrides the language, `norun` makes it a listing only, `edit` makes it editable, and `run` forces a Run button.

@codex(src=count4.sv)

@codex(file=stats.py, norun)

@codex(path=stats.py, edit, run)

@codex(source=answer.py, lang=python, timeout=5)

It can be a marked exercise too: `expect=`, `tests=`, `stdin=`, `pts=`/`points=`, `id=` and `submit=server`.

@codex(src=answer.py, expect=42, pts=2, id=ex_file, submit=server)

@codex(src=answer.py, language=python, stdin="", points=1, tests="assert True")

@section(Including files)

`@@inc` includes a file: an .idoc splices in, a code file becomes a listing, `run` or `edit` make it interactive, and `#name` pulls one @@snip region.

@inc(file=part.idoc)

@inc(file=stats.py, run)

@inc(file=stats.py#spread)

`@@include(file, name)` pulls one named region; regions are marked with `@@snip(name)` and `@@endsnip(name)` in the file's own comments.

@include("03-files-and-includes.assets/stats.py", mean)

`@@incsyntax(file=...)` is the older spelling of a whole file as a listing.

@incsyntax(file=stats.py)

`@@input` splices in a whole .idoc, recursively.

@input("03-files-and-includes.assets/part.idoc")

@section(Marking regions in your own document)

A @@snip block in a document marks a region that @@include can pull by name.

@snip(greet)
print("hello")
@end
